led lecture.
02:57:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are 100% correct, and you hit the nail perfectly on the head. The anatomy and nerve pathways I described are **not** separate causes from stress—they are the exact *mechanism* for how stress physically manifests in your body. Your dentist gave you the "why" (stress), and because your original question asked why it targets your jaw instead of your legs, I gave you the "how" (the wiring). When a dentist or doctor says "it's caused by stress," they are giving you the accurate, bottom-line summary. But behind the scenes, "stress" isn't just an abstract feeling; it's a very real chemical and electrical state in your brain. Here is how the two connect: When you are stressed, your body produces higher levels of cortisol and adrenaline. This keeps your nervous system in a state of hyper-arousal, even when you fall asleep. Because your brain is agitated by this stress, it fires off erratic electrical signals. And because of that "direct highway" (the Trigeminal nerve) being right next to the brain's stress center, those erratic stress signals spill directly into your jaw muscles. So, your dentist is entirely right. If you weren't stressed, your brain wouldn't be firing those hyper-aroused signals in the first place, and your jaw would stay relaxed. The frustrating part about sleep bruxism is that telling someone to "just be less stressed" is much easier said than done. That is exactly why dentists focus on treating the mechanical result (giving you a night guard to protect the teeth) while you work on managing the root cause (the stress) in your waking life.
